  "track.cleancode.m.fits.title": "One long function, or a few small ones read top-down",
  "track.cleancode.m.fits.overview": "ClinicBook's bookAppointment function checks the input, computes the fee, saves the booking and schedules the reminder. Read it part by part and count two things the way Seemann does: the number of paths through the code (cyclomatic complexity) and the number of variables to keep track of. In the last step a new fee requirement is added: see what it breaks in each version.",
  "track.cleancode.m.fits.wrapup": "The long function is only 19 lines, under Seemann's 24-line threshold, yet it has 9 paths and 13 variables: too much to hold in your head at once. Whoever edits the fee part adds an early return and does not see that it skips the reminder at the end. Split into a few small functions, each has 2 or 3 paths and at most 6 variables; the new requirement touches only feeFor, and the reminder lives elsewhere, so it cannot be skipped.",
  "track.cleancode.m.fits.simp.1": "The real function in the leading bug is 120 lines; the simulation shortens it to 19 so it fits on screen. The threshold of 7 is Seemann's symbolic number, not a hard limit of the brain.",
  "track.cleancode.m.fits.simp.2": "The counting is the site's convention following Seemann's method: start at 1, add 1 for each if and for. The code uses only if and for so the number does not depend on the tool; ?:, && and || also branch, but tools count them differently.",
